<section class="section section--soft">
  <div class="shell split split--sticky">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">Situation</p>
      <h2>A geriatric ward is the hard case.</h2>
    </div>
    <div>
      <p>
        Geriatric care concentrates everything that makes the old nurse call expensive. Patients
        press often and for a wide spread of reasons, from a genuine emergency to help getting to
        the bathroom. Many requests need a specific qualification; many others need none at all, but
        nobody on the receiving end can tell which is which.
      </p>
      <p>
        The clinic's existing system did what every nurse call does: it announced a room. Staff had
        no way to prepare, so a large share of responses ended with somebody walking back out of the
        room to fetch something or to fetch somebody.
      </p>
      <p>
        The ward's own sense was that this was costing real time. What nobody had was a number,
        because a bell cannot produce one.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="shell split split--sticky">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">What we deployed</p>
      <h2>Bed devices, nurse devices, and a log.</h2>
    </div>
    <div>
      <p>
        Bed devices let patients raise a specific need instead of an anonymous call, with a separate
        emergency action that always went out at top priority. Staff on shift carried nurse devices
        that listed the open requests for the ward, each with room, bed, the need itself, and how
        long it had been waiting.
      </p>
      <p>
        Behind both, every event was logged: what was raised, when, which qualification the request
        called for, and the distance the response involved. That log — not the devices — is the part
        of the pilot that changed the conversation with the ward.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

